  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...One last warm day on tap today before showers, \npossibly a few thunderstorms, return this afternoon and evening.\nExpecting temperatures to drop for Saturday behind the cold \nfront.\n\nAlready seeing quite a bit of clearing skies on the satellite \nthis morning; however, dewpoints remain in the 62 to 65 degree \nrange, only 1 or 2 degrees lower than the actual temperatures \naround southern New England, and combined with mostly calm \nwinds... wouldn't be surprised to see a little bit of patchy, \nshallow fog to develop in some areas.\n\nExpecting skies to stay mostly clear through the noon hour \ntoday, perhaps into the early afternoon, before the cold front \nstarts to push into NW MA. Once the sun comes up, expecting to \nsee the surface temperatures rising rapidly, to near or just \nabove 80F, due to those 925mb temperatures hovering in the +20C \nto +22C range.\n\nThe latest hi-res guidance is in good agreement that the cold \nfront, and the subsequent showers, should start to arrive around\n3 to 4 PM and exit offshore around 10 PM to midnight tonight. \nGiven the high dewpoints, PWATs are also expected to surge to \nnear 2.0\" (although the NAM is much more robust, in the \n2.0\"-2.25\" range along the immediate frontal boundary), and the \nlow-level lapse rates remain on the steeper side, near 7C/km. \nThis leads to the potential for some localized heavy downpours \nas the line of showers passes. While instability is on the lower\nend with only a few hundred J/kg of CAPE, it doesn't take much \nthis time of year to spark a few rumbles of thunder.\n\nBehind the cold front, a rather strong continental high \npressure system will build over the Great Lakes and southern \nOntario, sinking those +4C to +6C 850mb temperatures down into \nNew England and sharply dropping surface temperatures through \nthe weekend. Saturday's highs are likely to be on the order of \n10-15 degrees less than today's with overnight temps dropping \ninto the low 40s (low 50s on the Cape and Islands).\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Low potential for a few scattered showers \nSunday night south of I-95. A secondary, sharp temperature drop \nis then expected for the start of next week.\n\nStill quite a bit of uncertainty on if showers will push far \nenough north into southern New England Sunday evening. The \nlatest ECMWF and Canadian runs indicate some scattered showers \nbrushing across the Cape/Islands and immediate south coast while\nthe GFS shows widespread showers reaching all the way into \nsouthern NH. Opted to keep low-end PoPs south of I-95 for now.\n\nRegardless of the showers, a strong high pressure system is \nexpected to build in right on the heels of a deep Canadian low, \nbringing a secondary shot of even colder air to New England for \nthe start of next work week. Latest guidance indicates the 530mb\nline sinking possibly as far south as the Hartford-Providence-\nPlymouth corridor, meaning 850mb temperatures could drop to near\nor just below 0C by Tuesday morning. This would support surface\nhighs in mid 50s with nighttime lows possibly falling into the \n30s through Wednesday.\n\nThe high then pushes east by Thursday, marking the return of \npredominantly southwest flow and seasonable temperature to \nsouthern New England for the end of the week.",
